{
  "needed": true,
  "prompt": "Scope decision
- Scope: frontend changes only, limited to client-side handling of single-item resource operations (GET, PUT, DELETE) and centralized HTTP/error handling used by those calls. Do not change handling for authentication errors (401) or validation errors (422). Do not assume any other server-side contract changes.

Implementation tasks (high level)
1) Identify code paths to change
   - API client methods that fetch a single item or perform update/delete by item id.
   - Global HTTP error handler / interceptor (e.g., axios interceptor, fetch wrapper, common response parser).\n   - UI error-display components/utilities that read response bodies (toasts, inline field/error banners).
   - Unit/integration tests for item endpoints.

2) Error parsing rules to implement
   - Branch first on HTTP status code, not only on JSON shape.
   - For status === 404 (GET/PUT/DELETE of single item): attempt to parse JSON and interpret an object of the exact form { "error": { "code": "ITEM_NOT_FOUND", "message": "Item not found" } } as the canonical item-not-found signal. If parsed JSON is absent or does not match this structure, fall back to existing legacy behavior for 404.
   - For status === 403 (GET/PUT/DELETE of single item): attempt to parse JSON and interpret { "error": { "code": "ITEM_FORBIDDEN", "message": "Not enough permissions" } } as the canonical permission-denied signal. If parsed JSON is absent or does not match this structure, fall back to existing legacy behavior for 403.
   - For status === 401 (authentication error) and status === 422 (validation error): do not change existing parsing or UI behavior. Continue to use the pre-existing structure/fields your app depends on.
   - For any other status codes: preserve current error handling.

3) Specific parsing and fallback behavior (precise)
   - Always inspect response.status first.
   - On 404 or 403: try to parse response body as JSON. If parsing fails (not valid JSON or empty body), use the pre-existing 404/403 handling (do not treat it as the new structure).
   - If JSON parsed and contains an object at top-level property "error" with a string "code":
       - If status === 404 AND error.code === "ITEM_NOT_FOUND": treat as item-not-found. Prefer error.message for display, but if absent, fall back to a local default message exactly matching your UX needs.
       - If status === 403 AND error.code === "ITEM_FORBIDDEN": treat as permission-denied. Prefer error.message for display, fallback as above.
       - If status === 404 or 403 but error.code is present and not one of the above, do not coerce. Fall back to legacy handling.
   - Do not attempt to interpret other top-level shapes (for example, do not look for top-level detail for these two cases). The new item-not-found and item-forbidden payloads will not include a top-level detail; remove any code that assumes detail for these two codes.

4) UI update guidance
   - Where UI components display server error text for item endpoints, prefer the message from response.error.message for 403/404 when present. If the response body is missing or does not match, keep the existing display flow (e.g., existing localized/templated messages).
   - Do not change how authentication (401) and validation (422) messages are displayed.

5) Tests to add/modify
   - Unit tests for the API client/interceptor:
       a) Mock a 404 response with body { "error": { "code": "ITEM_NOT_FOUND", "message": "Item not found" } } and assert that codepath identifies ITEM_NOT_FOUND and returns/throws the expected frontend error object/value.
       b) Mock a 403 response with body { "error": { "code": "ITEM_FORBIDDEN", "message": "Not enough permissions" } } and assert permission-denied handling.
       c) Mock a 404/403 with non-JSON or different JSON shape and assert fallback to legacy 404/403 handling.
       d) Ensure existing 401 and 422 tests still pass; do not alter their expectations.
   - Integration/e2e tests: simulate user flows hitting item GET/PUT/DELETE that produce 403/404 and ensure UI shows expected messages or navigates accordingly.

6) Monitoring and rollout
   - Deploy frontend changes together with tests. Instrument metrics/logging for unexpected parsing failures (e.g., 403/404 with malformed body) so you can detect server responses that do not match the stated new contract.
   - If you have feature-flagging for new client behavior, enable it only for a subset and monitor. Otherwise run the standard release and monitor error rates.

7) Do NOT do
   - Do not change server contracts; these instructions assume server will return the two new structured error bodies for item GET/PUT/DELETE when applicable.
   - Do not forcibly re-shape all error responses into this new structure; only 403/404 for single-item endpoints should be handled as above. Authentication (401) and validation (422) must keep their existing structure and parsing.
   - Do not assume other error codes or fields beyond what is specified.

Practical example implementations
- Axios response interceptor (TypeScript-like pseudocode):
const axiosClient = axios.create(/* existing config */);
axiosClient.interceptors.response.use(
  response => response,
  async error => {
    const resp = error.response;
    if (!resp) return Promise.reject(error); // network error, keep existing behavior

    const status = resp.status;
    // only attempt special parsing for item endpoints or when status is 403/404
    if (status === 404 || status === 403) {
      // attempt safe JSON parse
      let body = null;
      try {
        body = typeof resp.data === 'object' ? resp.data : JSON.parse(resp.data);
      } catch (e) {
        // parsing failed: fall back to legacy handling
        return Promise.reject(error);
      }

      if (body && body.error && typeof body.error.code === 'string') {
        const code = body.error.code;
        const message = body.error.message || null;
        if (status === 404 && code === 'ITEM_NOT_FOUND') {
          // produce a typed frontend error or reject with a sentinel
          return Promise.reject({ type: 'ITEM_NOT_FOUND', status, message, raw: body });
        }
        if (status === 403 && code === 'ITEM_FORBIDDEN') {
          return Promise.reject({ type: 'ITEM_FORBIDDEN', status, message, raw: body });
        }
        // code present but unexpected: fall back to legacy handling
        return Promise.reject(error);
      }
      // no error.code present: fall back to legacy handling
      return Promise.reject(error);
    }

    // non-403/404: preserve existing behavior
    return Promise.reject(error);
  }
);

- Fetch wrapper example (JavaScript-like pseudocode):
async function handleItemResponse(response) {
  if (response.status === 404 || response.status === 403) {
    let body;
    try {
      body = await response.json();
    } catch (e) {
      // JSON parse failed: fall back to legacy behavior
      throw new Error('LEGACY_404_OR_403');
    }

    if (body && body.error && typeof body.error.code === 'string') {
      if (response.status === 404 && body.error.code === 'ITEM_NOT_FOUND') {
        throw { type: 'ITEM_NOT_FOUND', message: body.error.message || 'Item not found', raw: body };
      }
      if (response.status === 403 && body.error.code === 'ITEM_FORBIDDEN') {
        throw { type: 'ITEM_FORBIDDEN', message: body.error.message || 'Not enough permissions', raw: body };
      }
      // unknown code: fall back
      throw new Error('LEGACY_404_OR_403');
    }
    // no recognized body: fall back
    throw new Error('LEGACY_404_OR_403');
  }

  if (response.status === 401 || response.status === 422) {
    // keep existing parsing/throwing behavior for auth and validation
    return existingAuthAndValidationParser(response);
  }

  if (!response.ok) {
    // existing error handling for other status codes
    return existingGenericErrorParser(response);
  }

  return response.json();
}

Notes and rationale
- The client MUST branch on HTTP status code first to avoid incorrectly imposing the new structure on authentication/validation errors that intentionally keep their prior shapes.
- The client MUST treat the presence of error.code as authoritative for 403/404 only when status code matches and the code is exactly ITEM_NOT_FOUND or ITEM_FORBIDDEN. All other cases should fall back to legacy behavior.
- Remove any special-case code that reads top-level detail for these 404/403 cases; server will not include top-level detail for the new item errors.

Acceptance criteria (for QA/dev)
- For item GET/PUT/DELETE:
  - When server returns 404 with { "error": { "code": "ITEM_NOT_FOUND", "message": "Item not found" } }, frontend shows/handles item-not-found using error.message (or default fallback) and any downstream flows (redirects, empty-state) behave as expected.
  - When server returns 403 with { "error": { "code": "ITEM_FORBIDDEN", "message": "Not enough permissions" } }, frontend shows/handles permission-denied using error.message (or default fallback) and blocks the action accordingly.
  - When server returns 401 or 422, existing authentication and validation flows remain unchanged.
  - When server returns 404/403 with non-JSON or unexpected JSON, the existing legacy handling is used and no crash occurs.

Deliverables
- Updated API client / interceptor implementing the above logic.
- Updated unit and integration tests covering positive and fallback cases.
- Small UI changes where needed to prefer error.error.message for 403/404.
- Monitoring dashboard entries/alerts for any unexpected 403/404 response shapes.

Do not assume any other server-side changes beyond the two specific structured error bodies and their HTTP status codes as described."
}
